From 235477c0962326cff14af5645c1e51cc80c16370 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 03:55:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 15/20] relay_metrics: Count circuits and directory streams by
 previous hop

Under a BEGIN_DIR flood, the network team asks whether the streams are
onion service requests or ordinary directory requests, and whether
their circuits come over authenticated channels (from relays) or
unauthenticated ones (from tor clients, one hop), because the two call
for different fixes. The metrics answered the second question only for
onion service requests refused as single hop: ordinary requests,
streams that never sent a request and CREATE cells carried no previous
hop, and nothing counted BEGIN_DIR cells per circuit.

Classify a circuit's previous hop when its CREATE cell arrives, with
the new channel_get_prev_hop(): "client" (the channel never
authenticated: a tor client or a bridge), "known_relay" (it
authenticated as a relay in our consensus, or whose descriptor we
have), or "unknown_relay" (it authenticated with an identity we don't
know). This is how the previous hop identified itself, not a hop count.
The circuit keeps it in two bits, and its directory connections copy
it, so a request is attributed even after its circuit is gone.

Count, by that class:
- tor_relay_create_cell_total: every CREATE cell, before we decide
  whether to accept it;
- tor_relay_begindir_cell_total, also by "nth", the cell's place among
  its circuit's BEGIN_DIR cells (1, 2_10, 11_100, 101_1000,
  1001_plus): every well-formed BEGIN_DIR cell for a new stream, before
  tor decides whether to refuse it;
- tor_relay_begindir_request_total, also by "type": each tunneled
  directory request, once, by what it asked for: hs_get, hs_post,
  consensus, microdesc, descriptor, other (including 404s), unparsed,
  no_request if the stream closed without sending anything, or
  circuit_dead if we dropped it unread because its stream or circuit was
  already closing. Its sum by previous hop is the accepted BEGIN_DIR
  streams, which tor_relay_streams_total{type="BEGIN_DIR"} already
  counts in total.

tor_relay_begindir_request_total now counts onion service requests too,
and its type "non_hs" is split into the types it summed. Its type
"unparsed" no longer includes the requests dropped unread because their
stream or circuit was closing, such as those queued on a circuit closed
for its second HSDir request: they are "circuit_dead".

The counters live in rephist.c, which is always built, so command.c and
connection_edge.c need no new includes. The cost is one known-relay
lookup per CREATE cell from an authenticated channel, twelve bits per
circuit and three per directory connection (neither struct grows), and
43 more series. hsdir_unknown_prev_hop keeps its lookup per request, so
it still matches what HSDirRefuseUnknownPrevHop refuses.

Part of #41339.
